<div class="article-body">

<p>My first batch of hydroponic lettuce grew beautifully for 10 days. Dark green leaves, strong stems, I thought I was a natural. Then day 12 hit and everything stopped. The leaves turned pale yellow at the edges. The growth stalled completely.</p>

<p>I spent $35 on extra nutrients. the "bloom booster," the "root stimulator," the "cal-mag." I dumped it all in. Within 3 days, the leaves curled brown and the roots started looking slimy. I'd spent an extra $35 and made everything 10x worse.</p>

<p>After a month of failing, I was ready to scrap the whole setup and go back to buying $4 bags at the store. But then I found the problem. And it was so simple that I spent 3 weeks feeling stupid about it.</p>

<p>That first discovery saved batch 1. But the real challenge came later. and it cost me 2 more batches before I understood the full picture.</p>

<p>Here's what I learned after losing 3 harvests of hydroponic lettuce. If you're reading this because your lettuce is stunted, yellow, or just refusing to grow. start here before you waste any more money on bottled fixes.</p>

<p>Full disclosure: I'm not a botanist. I'm just someone who killed lettuce 3 times before figuring out how to keep it alive.</p>

<h2 id="why">1. Why Your Hydroponic Lettuce Stops Growing</h2>

<p>I spent a month treating the symptoms instead of the cause. Yellow leaves? Add nutrients. Slow growth? More light. Purple stems? Definitely a deficiency. I was wrong every single time.</p>

<p>Here's what took me 3 weeks to figure out: <strong>the #1 cause of stunted lettuce isn't nutrients. It's pH.</strong> Lettuce is pickier about pH than almost any other hydroponic crop. It needs to stay between 5.5 and 6.0. Outside that range, it stops absorbing nutrients entirely. even if you're dumping in enough fertilizer for a tomato plant.</p>

<p>I checked my pH after 3 weeks of failure. It was at 7.2. My lettuce was starving in a pool of perfect nutrients it couldn't absorb. I adjusted it to 5.8 and within 48 hours, I saw the first new green growth in 3 weeks.</p>

<p>I thought I'd cracked it. I was wrong.</p>

<h2 id="check-1">2. The First Mistake: I Overcorrected Everything</h2>

<p>After fixing the pH on batch 1, I started batch 2 with total confidence. I checked pH every morning. I followed the nutrient chart perfectly. For 2 weeks, everything looked great. Then the leaves started curling at the edges.</p>

<p>I was so sure it couldn't be pH again that I ignored it for 4 days. By the time I tested, the pH was fine. 5.8. The EC was at 1200. I was following the chart for tomatoes, not lettuce. Lettuce is a light feeder. it needs about half the nutrients of most other plants.</p>

<p>I was feeding my lettuce like it was a fruiting plant. The roots went slimy in a week. If your lettuce leaves are curling down at the tips, this is probably you.</p>

<p>Here's the truth about hydroponic lettuce: <strong>less is more.</strong> Target 400-600 PPM after the first 2 weeks. Start at quarter strength. Wait 3 days. If the leaves are light green, bump it up slightly. Dark green means you're at the right level. If the tips curl or burn, you're already too high.</p>

<h2 id="check-2">3. The One That Almost Made Me Quit: Hidden Root Rot</h2>

<p>By batch 3, I was confident. pH was at 5.8. EC at 500. I was checking everything twice a day. The water was clear, the roots looked fine. I even told a friend "I finally figured this out." That's when it all fell apart.</p>

<p>The lettuce stayed alive, but it refused to grow. For 10 days, zero new leaves. I tested everything again. pH, nutrients, light. all perfect. I was ready to give up.</p>

<p>I pulled a plant out of its net cup to check the roots one more time. That's when I saw it: the roots weren't bright white. They were beige. Slightly translucent. Not slimy, but clearly not healthy.</p>

<p>This is <strong>early-stage root rot</strong>. the most deceptive problem in hydroponics. The water looks fine. The roots don't look rotten. But they've stopped absorbing nutrients. Your lettuce is sipping through a clogged straw.</p>

<p>The cause was water temperature. My reservoir was at 74°F. Lettuce roots prefer 60-68°F. At 74°F, dissolved oxygen drops and bacteria multiply faster. The roots slowly suffocate over 2 weeks before you see any visible rot.</p>

<p>I dropped a frozen water bottle in the reservoir twice a day. Within 4 days, new white roots started growing and the lettuce finally grew its first new leaf in two weeks.</p>

<p>This was the most expensive mistake. not in money, but in time. I lost 3 full weeks of growth because I didn't check water temperature.</p>

<h2 id="check-3">4. Check #3: Light That Looks Bright Enough (But Isn't)</h2>

<p>This one almost made me quit. After fixing pH, nutrients, and root temperature, my fourth batch of lettuce should have been perfect. But the leaves were still pale and leggy.</p>

<p>I checked everything again. Then I put my phone above the canopy and checked the light reading. My light was 18 inches above the plants. For lettuce, LED strips should be 6-8 inches above the canopy. I moved it down to 6 inches and within 3 days the new leaves were noticeably darker and wider.</p>

<p>Lettuce is forgiving about light quality. it'll grow under almost any spectrum. But it's NOT forgiving about light distance. Too far and the plant stretches toward the light, producing thin, pale leaves with long spaces between nodes. If your lettuce looks like it's reaching upward instead of growing outward, lower your light.</p>

<p>For a full guide on grow lights for leafy greens, check the <a href="https://growlightdb.tabletopharvest.com" target="_blank">Grow Light DB</a> to compare options by coverage area and heat output.</p>

<h2 id="fix">5. The 48-Hour Fix Checklist</h2>

<p>If your hydroponic lettuce stopped growing, here's the exact order I test now. It saves me hours of guessing.</p>

<p><strong>Step 1: Check pH.</strong> If it's above 6.5, that's your problem. Use <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=pH+test+kit+hydroponics+drops&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">pH test drops</a> for accuracy. they're $8 and never need calibration. Bring it down to 5.5-6.0 with <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=General+Hydroponics+pH+Down&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">pH Down solution</a> ($10). Wait 24 hours. If you see new growth, you found it.</p>

<p><strong>Step 2: Check EC/PPM.</strong> For lettuce, target 400-600 PPM (0.8-1.2 EC) after the seedling stage. If you're above 800, dilute your reservoir by 25% with plain pH-balanced water. If you're below 300, add a light dose of <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=General+Hydroponics+FloraSeries+nutrient+kit&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">General Hydroponics FloraSeries</a> ($35 starter kit) at quarter strength.</p>

<p><strong>Step 3: Check water temp.</strong> Put a thermometer in your reservoir. If it's above 72°F, drop in a frozen water bottle. Swap twice a day. Your lettuce will thank you within 48 hours.</p>

<p><strong>Step 4: Check light distance.</strong> Your light should be 6-8 inches from the top of the canopy. If you need a light that runs cool enough to sit that close, the <a href="https://growlightdb.tabletopharvest.com" target="_blank">Grow Light DB</a> has options tested for leafy greens.</p>

<p><strong>Step 5: Inspect roots.</strong> Pull a plant out of its net cup. Healthy roots are bright white. If they're beige or translucent, you have early root rot. Add an air stone if you don't have one, or lower your water temperature.</p>

